`wicketd` is the backend service that wicket will interact with to provide technician port based operator functionality to the rack. This is the start of an implementation and there is much more to do. Currently, `wicketd` is essentially a caching MGS proxy for the `/sp` endpoint of MGS. The MGS endpoint is paginated, but for wicket, we don't really mind waiting as we plan to poll for changes periodically. To ensure we get all information, we just attempt to retrieve the max amount of components (36) within a given timeout. This limits us to one page that wicket then stores in memory and serves as `/inventory`.

There is an open TODO to fetch the SP inventory for each SP retrieved from `/sp` via the MGS `/sp/{type}/{slot}/component` endpoint. Once this is implemented, wicket will be modified to talk to wicketd and serve this inventory instead of the fake inventory currently there.

Since we want to allow proxying information from MGS with a minimum of hassle, `gateway-client` was changed to derive `schemars::JsonSchema` for generated types so those could be served from `wicketd`.

Since this is a caching proxy for inventory, when wicket users request inventory a request gets forwarded over a channel to the `MgsManager` which returns an `Arc<RackV1Inventory>`. This prevents the need to copy this data. When the inventory gets updated a new `Arc<RackV1Inventory>` is created and will be served over the same channel. This channel based mechanism works nicely, but it's worth noting the alternative of using an `Arc<Mutex<` or `<Arc<RwLock`, or even a
`tokio::sync::watch::channel`. There may be performance tradeoffs with them, but the behavior is generally the same, and I find the message passing flow easier to follow in most cases. I'm open to changing this down the road.

There is also an intention to support ETAGs for the inventory, which would negate the need to bother with an `Arc` when sending the message over a channel, as copying would be so rare. This is left for a follow up commit, although there is some embedded support.

It should also be noted that polling the SPs every 10 seconds and retrieving inventory is really inefficient. Instead MGS should maintain a generation count of when inventory changes and provide information about what changed. Alternatively, MGS could also use ETAGs and just change the ETAG for `/sp` when anything changes. This would have more overhead, but *may* be simpler to implement. In order to implement either of these optimizations however, there would need to be some SP support for keeping track of changes. It's worth trying to determine when inventory can even change. Is it during upgrades or insertion of new hardware only?

